  "openQuestions": [
    {
      "id": "OQ-1",
      "q": "Does CHK-001's canonical shared-root resolution (a direct `git rev-parse --git-common-dir` call, per the story's own chosen option) satisfy or violate the repo's written design principle to reuse the existing signed-workspace resolver instead of adding a second independent root-resolution mechanism? git-common-dir (shared .git dir across linked worktrees of one clone) and signedWorkspaceRoot/gitRoot (metadataRoot vs wrapper-root git material split) resolve different concepts, so they may be legitimately complementary rather than duplicative, but no decisions/ doc reconciles this for the validation-lock use case.",
      "evidence": "\"6. Reuse signed workspace resolution instead of adding a second root resolver.\" and (Delivered notes) \"gitRoot bootstrap reuses the signed-workspace resolver — dev_start (handlers/worktree.ts) and the INIT_CHECK metadata reconcile (cli-engine.ts) resolve the git root via resolveStorySignedWorkspace (the same resolver session_brief uses), never a second resolver.\"",
      "sourcePointers": [
        "packages/main/src/core/story-signed-workspace-resolution.ts",
        "CHK-001"
      ],
      "resolution": "Reuse resolveStorySignedWorkspace to resolve the story gitRoot, then derive the SHARED lock root via git rev-parse --git-common-dir from that gitRoot. This is complementary, not a second resolver: resolveStorySignedWorkspace returns a per-worktree gitRoot (different per linked worktree), whereas git-common-dir returns the SAME path for all linked worktrees of one repo (verified: main/.worktrees/B-231d/.worktrees/B-956f all resolve to /Users/jek/Documents/Work/proofbound/.git). The lock must key on the shared common-dir; we keep resolveStorySignedWorkspace as the single gitRoot entry point and add only the common-dir derivation, honoring the reuse-don't-duplicate principle.",
      "resolvedAt": "2026-07-18T23:05:01.373Z"
    },
    {
      "id": "OQ-2",
      "q": "scope-boundary.json lists \"S1 orchestrator routing\" as an explicit exclusion for B-231d, but this term does not appear anywhere in the enumerated 121-file decisions/ corpus (including the story's own referenced spec, repro-first-gate-enforce-execution-plan.md). Is this exclusion grounded in a decisions/ doc not yet written, or is it a mislabel/carry-over from another story's scope contract?",
      "evidence": "\"excluded\": [\"S1 orchestrator routing\", \"W1 language-neutral classifier\", \"W2 RED evidence subsystem\", \"W3 enforce gate\"]",
      "sourcePointers": [
        "docs/requirements/decisions/repro-first-gate-enforce-execution-plan.md"
      ],
      "resolution": "The scope-boundary excluded entry 'S1 orchestrator routing' is a PROGRAM-internal cross-reference to sibling story B-d877 (route dev_finish/dev_merge through runStoryTransition), part of the same Workstream-0 pre-existing-defects table in docs/requirements/decisions/repro-first-gate-enforce-execution-plan.md; it is not a decisions/-corpus feature term. This story (B-231d = W0.2 lock + W0.4 digest) deliberately excludes the orchestrator-routing defect (W0.1) and the same-owner mutex (W0.3), which are owned by B-d877; they are orphan-suspect here only because the split is program-level, not corpus-level.",
      "resolvedAt": "2026-07-18T23:05:02.961Z"
    }
  ]
